==English==
{{wp}}

===Etymology===
{{root|en|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-}}
From {{inh|en|enm|weder}}, {{m|enm|wedir}}, from {{inh|en|ang|weder}}, from {{inh|en|gmw-pro|*wedr}}, from {{inh|en|gem-pro|*wedrą}}, from {{inh|en|ine-pro|*wedʰrom}} (={{m|ine-pro|*we-dʰrom}}), from {{m|ine-pro|*h₂weh₁-||to blow}}.

{{rel-top|Cognates}}
Cognate with {{cog|stq|Weeder}}, {{cog|fy|waar}}, {{cog|nl|weer}}, {{cog|nds|Weder}}, {{cog|de|Wetter}}, {{cog|da|vejr}}, {{cog|sv|väder}}, {{cog|nb|vær}}, {{cog|nn|vêr}}, {{cog|is|veður}}; also more distantly related to Russian {{m|ru|вёдро||fair weather}} and perhaps Albanian {{m|sq|vrëndë||light rain}}.

Other cognates include {{cog|sa|निर्वाण||blown or put out, extinguished}}.
{{rel-bottom}}

