===Verb===
{{en-verb}}

# To [[expose]] to the weather, or show the [[effect]]s of such exposure, or to [[withstand]] such effects.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1856|author=w:Hugh Miller|title=The Cruise of the Betsey
|passage=The organisms {{...}} seem indestructible, while the hard matrix in which they are embedded has '''weathered''' from around them.}}
#* {{RQ:Spenser Faerie Queene|book=V|canto=IV|stanza=42|passage=[An eagle] soaring through his wide empire of the air / To '''weather''' his broad sails.}}
# {{lb|en|by extension}} To sustain the trying effect of; to bear up against and overcome; to endure; to resist.
#* {{RQ:Longfellow Ballads|poem=The Wreck of the Hesperus|stanza=8|page=44|passage="Come hither! come hither! my little daughter, / And do not tremble so; / For I can '''weather''' the roughest gale, That ever wind did blow."}}
#* '''April 18, 1850''', {{w|Frederick William Robertson}}, ''An Address Delivered to the Members of the Working Man's Institute''
#*: You will '''weather''' the difficulties yet.
#* {{quote-web|en|date=15-11-2023|author=Tessa Wong|work=BBC|url=https://www.bbc.com/news/world-asia-china-67423040|title=Xi Jinping arrives in US as his Chinese Dream sputters|passage=Some observers have contrasted China with the US, whose economy has '''weathered''' the post-Covid recovery better. Until recently, Americans may have feared the day China would overtake them as the world's largest economy, but now analysts doubt if this will happen.}}
# To break down, of rocks and other materials, under the effects of exposure to rain, sunlight, temperature, and air.
# To cause (rocks) to break down by [[crush]]ing, [[grind]]ing, and/or [[dissolve|dissolving]] with [[acids]].
#* {{quote-book|en|year=2020| title=Entangled Life|author=Merlin Sheldrake| page=85| passage=Lichens' ability to '''weather''' makes them a geological force, yet they do more than disolve the physical features of the world.}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To pass to [[windward]] in a vessel, especially to [[beat]] 'round.
#: {{ux|en|to '''weather''' a cape&emsp;&emsp;&emsp; to '''weather''' another ship}}
# {{lb|en|nautical}} To [[endure]] or [[survive]] an event or action without [[undue]] [[damage]].
#: {{ux|en|Joshua '''weathered''' a collision with a freighter near South Africa.}}
# {{lb|en|falconry}} To place (a hawk) unhooded in the open air.
#* {{quote-book|en|year=1773|author=James Campbell|title=A Treatise on modern faulconry|passage=If your hawk is bad-'''weathered''', that is, will not fit on your fist when the wind blows, but hales, and beats, and hangs by the je{{long s}}{{long s}}es, {{long s}}he has an ill habit of the wor{{long s}}t kind.}}

